---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CardDetail from '../components/CardDetail.astro';
import CardMore from '../components/CardMore.astro';
import FamilyLine from '../components/FamilyLine.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import FeatureList from '../components/FeatureList.astro';
import WriteGate from '../components/WriteGate.astro';
import BadNotZero from '../components/BadNotZero.astro';
import { TIERS } from '../lib/tiers';
import { CURRENT, GATEWAY_FEATURES, product } from '../lib/products';

const protocols = (await getCollection('protocols')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const native = protocols.filter((p) => p.data.verification !== 'gateway-mode');
const releases = (await getCollection('releases'))
  .filter((r) => r.data.product === 'core')
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.getTime() - a.data.date.getTime() || b.data.version.localeCompare(a.data.version, undefined, { numeric: true }));
const latest = releases.find((r) => r.data.published)!;
const gw = product('gateway');
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop Gateway: OPC UA, Modbus and MQTT gateway"
  description="An industrial automation gateway with twelve native protocols, ISA-18.2 alarms, a historian, and one write gate that nothing can route around."
  ogAlt="HotLoop Gateway, an industrial automation gateway."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: 'HotLoop Gateway',
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/gateway/',
      description:
        'An industrial automation gateway that polls equipment over OPC UA, Modbus, MQTT and Sparkplug B, EtherNet/IP, S7comm, MTConnect, HTTP, SNMP, BACnet/IP, and a UniFi network, keeps the history, raises ISA-18.2 alarms, and exposes the plant to agents over MCP.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      softwareVersion: CURRENT,
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
    <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">HotLoop Gateway</span><span class="chip ok">{latest.data.version} is out</span></div>
      <h1>Every reading is real. Every write is earned.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The Gateway runs a whole site from one box. It connects to your PLCs, drives and meters over the protocols they
        already speak, keeps every value with its quality, alarms to ISA-18.2, and rolls up every site onto one screen.
        A dead sensor shows as a dead sensor, and nothing moves a machine until someone with the authority arms it.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>{latest.data.version} is out. It is four products from one release, agents can write behind the same gate as the screen, a scaled tag takes the value you meant, and a CODESYS runtime is read by its own variable names.</strong>
          <p>
            It installs on any Kubernetes with one chart, or on a plain Linux box, and both pull with no login. Already
            running an older version? <a href="#upgrading">Upgrading is a short read</a>, and the docs carry the step by step.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install it</a>
        <a class="btn" href="#the-gate">Try to break the write gate</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/releases/core/">Release notes</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://docs.hotloop.io/gateway/overview/">Docs</a>
      </div>
      <FamilyLine id="gateway" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Where it sits</span>
        <h2>The top of the lineup. Everything, for the whole site.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          HotLoop is one codebase shipped as four products, all in the same release.
          {' '}<a href="/iot/">IoT</a> is the automation base, <a href="/edge/">Edge</a> adds the machine layer, and
          the Gateway is both of those plus what a whole site needs: fleet management, multi-site roll-ups and
          scheduled reports. The <a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a> is the one with no screen that polls and forwards.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          All four are out in {latest.data.version}. If you need one box running a whole
          site, this is the box. If the box sits next to one machine, <a href="/edge/">Edge</a> is smaller.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">What only the Gateway has</span>
        <FeatureList features={GATEWAY_FEATURES} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="the-gate">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">The write gate</span>
        <h2>Every write goes through one gate, and nothing routes around it.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The screen, a rule, a script, an agent: same gate, same checks, same order. Policy lives in one
          place because the day it lives in five, somebody forgets the fifth, and that somebody's write goes to a
          live press.
        </p>
      </div>
      <WriteGate />
      <p class="prose muted after">
        An agent gets no side door. It hits the same gate an operator
        does, and it can't write without a stated reason. The audit row records who and what, and the reason lands
        in the system log right next to the write it explains. Six months later, every write still has its reason in
        the log.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Bad is not zero</span>
        <h2>A failed read is not a value.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Every reading carries good, uncertain or bad. A driver that can't read a point still reports, marked bad,
          because silence looks exactly like a value that hasn't changed. Alarms hold their state on a bad reading
          instead of evaluating it, so a dead instrument shows up as a dead instrument and not as a process upset.
        </p>
      </div>
      <BadNotZero />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Built for agents</span>
        <h2>An assistant that can read everything and touch only what you armed.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Over a hundred tools let an AI agent read the plant and, behind a switch, change its configuration, with the
          permission of whoever it signs in as and a required reason. An
          agent reads the plant in entities, the infeed pump rather than register 40001, and it can only act through
          the gate. So you can ask it why line 3 is slow and let it read everything, knowing it can only touch what
          you armed, and every write it makes comes with a reason in the log.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          It goes the other way too: a rule can call a tool on another agent server.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Many sites</span>
        <h2>One question, every plant.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Point one Gateway at the others and the Sites screen asks each of them the question it would answer
          itself. Each site keeps its own database, so a dead WAN link takes out a roll-up, never a plant.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          A site that doesn't answer shows as unreachable, with the reason, and stays out of the totals. Counting a
          plant you can't reach as zero alarms is how a head office decides everything is fine on the worst night
          of the year.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What it speaks</span>
        <h2>Twelve native drivers, and a table that admits how far each has been proven.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-4">
        {native.map((p) => (
          <a class="card proto reveal" href={`/integrations/${p.id}/`}>
            <div class="cluster head">
              <h3>{p.data.name}</h3>
              <span class={`chip ${TIERS[p.data.verification].chip}`}>{TIERS[p.data.verification].label}</span>
            </div>
            <p>{p.data.summary}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="notice info after">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>S7comm and EtherNet/IP have run on bare metal (2026-11-17).</strong>
          <p>
            As with any new controller, confirm a value you already know at commissioning.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="notice after">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0z" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>SNMP and BACnet/IP are tested on bare metal (2026-11-17).</strong>
          <p>
            SNMP was read at every v3 security level, and BACnet/IP against an independent BACnet stack. Both have also run on bare metal.
            Vendors differ about their MIBs, their object lists, and how they answer a request that is too big, so a new switch, UPS, or controller is still a commissioning job: compare the probe's values with the device's own display.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What's inside</span>
        <h2>The rest of the plant, in one program.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          No sidecar, no second service to keep alive, no runtime to babysit. One self-contained program that runs as a
          non-root user with every special privilege dropped, so there is nothing extra to patch and nothing extra to attack.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Historian</h3><p>TimescaleDB (a time-series database) when it's there, plain PostgreSQL partitioning when it isn't. Per-tag deadband, so a sensor twitching in its last bit doesn't write a row every scan until the disk fills.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Alarms</h3><p>ISA-18.2 (the alarm-management standard), including alarms that cleared before anyone acknowledged them. Acknowledged and cleared are separate, so the trip at 03:00 that fixed itself is still on the list when the morning shift walks in.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Automations</h3><p>Eleven kinds of trigger, entity state and the sun included, and conditions checked on save. A broken rule gets caught in the editor, not at 3am halfway through a batch.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>The Board and dashboards</h3><p>The Board is the screen that lives on a wall, with gauges that show the alarm limits where they fall. Build other dashboards for a role or a physical screen.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Entities</h3><p>The infeed pump, not register 40001. Every tag gets an entity, and a motor, valve, or PID loop is one entity built from the tags that make it up.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Plant tree</h3><p>One ISA-95 tree (the standard plant hierarchy), enterprise down to equipment, plus labels. Filter a whole area in one click, and the site, area, and line your devices already carry stay in step with it.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Paging</h3><p>ntfy, Gotify, Discord, webhook, and email. Acknowledge from an ntfy push or the Discord bot and the Gateway records it.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Reports</h3><p>Historian numbers on a schedule, as CSV or HTML, stored and emailed. Only good readings count, every row says how many it threw out, and an empty period says no data, never zero.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>OPC UA server</h3><p>Read-only, secured, and Ignition reads it end to end. The SCADA you already paid for can see every tag, and there is no way to write through it.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Edge Relay</h3><p>Its own product: about 15 MB against the Gateway's 35 on a Pi, no database, no UI. <a href="/edge-relay/">More on the Edge Relay</a>.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Embedded broker</h3><p>An optional MQTT broker in the same program, so a single box can be its own Sparkplug hub. At scale, run a real broker.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Backups</h3><p>One backup, taken in place. Restore loads an older backup cleanly and flat refuses one from a newer version, so a restore can't half-work.</p></article>
      </div>
      <CardDetail>
        <div><h4>The Board and dashboards</h4><p>The Board is the screen that lives on a wall: gauges with the alarm limits drawn where they fall, and a layout stored with the plant, so the panel PC and your laptop always show the same thing. It is one dashboard among the ones you can build, for a role or for a physical screen.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Entities</h4><p>The infeed pump, not register 40001. Every tag gets an entity for free, and a motor, valve, or PID loop is one entity built from the tags that make it up. Rules, screens and agents read like the plant, not like a register map.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Paging</h4><p>ntfy, Gotify, Discord, webhook, and email. Press Acknowledge on an ntfy push or in the Discord bot and the alarm is acknowledged in the Gateway, signed and good for one use.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Edge Relay</h4><p>Its own product: about 15 MB against the Gateway's 35 on a Pi, no database, no UI. The Gateway's fleet management tracks and commands every relay you register (no screen for it yet). <a href="/edge-relay/">More on the Edge Relay</a>.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Embedded broker</h4><p>An optional MQTT broker in the same program, so a single box can be its own Sparkplug hub. Logins made in HotLoop carry per-topic publish and subscribe rules. At scale, run a real broker.</p></div>
      </CardDetail>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Install</span>
        <h2>Helm, from the published chart.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Any Kubernetes, k3s included. The chart brings its own TimescaleDB, generates the database password, the
          agent token and an admin account, and keeps all three across upgrades, so an upgrade never logs you out of
          your own plant.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2 split">
        <div class="stack">
<pre><code>helm repo add hotloop https://hotloop.io/hotloop
helm repo update
helm install hotloop hotloop/hotloop --version {latest.data.version} \
  --namespace hotloop --create-namespace</code></pre>
          <p class="muted">
            The chart generates the admin password, keeps it in the release Secret, and
            the install notes print the command that reads it back. Open the Gateway in a browser, sign in, add a
            device, discover its tags.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="muted">
            Then notice that you can't write to anything. That's on purpose. Every tag starts read-only and gets armed
            one at a time, and the writes switch stays off until you decide this install gets to move machinery.
            Nobody's first day with HotLoop should end with a press doing something they didn't ask for.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            It runs on amd64 and arm64, it is about 37 MB, and it pulls with no login. The database is a setting:
            TimescaleDB by default, with plain PostgreSQL and SQLite in the same image. Each product picks its own. IoT
            takes SQLite or PostgreSQL. Edge and the Gateway take SQLite, PostgreSQL or TimescaleDB. The Edge Relay has no
            database at all.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            Off a cluster, the Gateway installs as plain container service files that start at boot and roll a bad
            update back. The {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/install/quadlet/">install guide</a> has them, and the
            {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/gateway/install/">install docs</a> cover the settings worth
            choosing before a real plant sees it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card upgrade reveal" id="upgrading">
        <span class="label">Already running HotLoop</span>
        <h3>Upgrading is a Helm upgrade, and the notes tell you what changes on a running plant.</h3>
        <p>Agents can write now, and a value written to a scaled tag lands in the register the way you meant it. Read both before you touch a running plant.</p>
        <CardMore label="What to read first">
          <p>
            <strong>Agents can write.</strong> Agent writes are on by default. A deployment that has writes on and never set
            the agent switch gets agent writes at the upgrade, through the same gate as the screen. One that saved it off
            stays off. The agent endpoint always has a token now, so an agent that connected without one stops until you
            hand it the token.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Scaled tags.</strong> Write 55.5 to a tag scaled by 0.1 and 555 lands in the register, where 55 used to.
            Anyone who typed raw counts to work around it now sends ten times too much, so look at every scaled tag that
            somebody writes by hand, from an automation or from a recipe, and change those numbers in the same step.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Coming from an older release.</strong> Each step has its own short checklist, from the database move to
            PostgreSQL 17 to the way device passwords are encrypted. Take a database backup first, like always. The
            {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/gateway/upgrading/">upgrade docs</a> walk every starting point, including
            4.18.11, 4.17.0, 4.16.0, and 4.3.1.
          </p>
        </CardMore>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="whats-new">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What changed lately</span>
        <h2>The detail is in the release notes, and it is one click away.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          {latest.data.version} is the current release. Lately: X.509 sign-in on the OPC UA server with the deprecated
          security policies off by default, the new web UI as the default, agents that can change everything they can read,
          tag pickers that search the whole plant, agents that write behind the same gate as the screen, scaled tags that
          land right, CODESYS read by variable name, Kafka and OPC UA PubSub, a database move to PostgreSQL 17 that runs itself,
          the four-product split with MQTT and network discovery, historian targets, boxes that update themselves, and recipes,
          scripts, blueprints, helpers, the logbook and read-only UniFi before that. Each release has its own page with what it
          buys you and what it costs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="more"><a class="btn" href="/releases/core/">Read the core release notes</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free for home and lab. Businesses run it on EmberNet.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            The Gateway is under the HotLoop Free Use License 1.0. It is free to use, it is not open source, and it
never converts to open source. There are no license keys and nothing unlocks at runtime. Home and lab use is free. Businesses
run it on {' '}<a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNet</a>, starting with a free Pilot.
            Support, SLAs and warranties come from our Official Systems Integrator,
            {' '}<a href="https://fireballz.ai">Fireball Industries</a>, who'll also design, install, and support the
            rollout if you'd rather not.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the license</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  .page-hero .notice code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .upgrade { margin-top: var(--hl-s7); display: grid; gap: var(--hl-s3); }
  .upgrade ol { padding-left: 1.3em; display: grid; gap: var(--hl-s2); color: var(--hl-ink-soft); font-size: var(--hl-fs-sm); }
  pre { max-width: 100%; }
  .after { margin-top: var(--hl-s6); }

  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .proto { text-decoration: none; color: inherit; display: block; transition: transform var(--hl-transition-fast), border-color var(--hl-transition-fast); }
  .proto:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); border-color: var(--hl-orange); }
  .proto .head { justify-content: space-between; align-items: start; margin-bottom: var(--hl-s2); }
  .proto h3 { font-size: var(--hl-fs-md); margin: 0; }

  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
